{
  "result": "Every morning, the bakery on the corner opens before the rest of the street remembers itself. Its windows glow amber while buses hiss past and the sky slowly changes its mind about rain. By seven, the first loaf has cooled enough to slice, and the neighborhood begins to gather outside.\n\nThere is a woman who buys one roll and feeds half to the pigeons, though a sign politely asks her not to. There is a student with ink on his fingers, a driver finishing a night shift, and a child convinced the cinnamon buns are tiny sleeping dragons in disguise.\n\nThe baker knows almost none of their names. Still, she notices when the driver looks tired, when the student has an exam, and when the child is missing a front tooth. She keeps the roundest bun aside for him, adding two raisins for eyes whenever his mother remembers to ask.\n\nOne winter morning, a storm knocks out the electricity. The ovens fall silent, the register goes dark, and the baker stands in the doorway wondering what to do. The driver brings a flashlight. The student finds candles. The woman returns with a thermos of tea and six mismatched paper cups.\n\nThey share yesterday\u2019s bread by candlelight, listening to rain drum on the awning. No one calls it a celebration, but the child names each candle after a dragon. Later, when the lights return, everyone applauds. The baker turns on the ovens, and the street begins again, somehow warmer than before.",
